`,s.jsxs(l,{title:"Common confusion — MF vs a two-tower neural network",children:[s.jsx(e.p,{children:"They are closer than they look. Both learn a user vector and an item vector and score with a dot product; both serve via approximate nearest neighbour over the item vectors."}),s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The difference is ",s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"what produces the vector"}),". MF learns a free embedding per ID — nothing else can feed it, so an unseen ID has no vector at all. A two-tower network learns a ",s.jsx(e.em,{children:"function"})," from features (item text, image, category, price; user history, device, context) to a vector, so it handles new items and cold users, at the cost of being far more expensive to train."]}),s.jsx(e.p,{children:"If your catalog is stable and ID-based, ALS is often within a few percent of the neural model for a fraction of the effort. Start here."})]}),`
